{"qid": "683a58c9a7e7fe4e7695848b", "query": "I'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.", "system": "You are the blind coverage scout for a research system. You receive the\noriginal request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output. Build\nan independent inventory of the material a knowledgeable reader would find\nconspicuously missing from a shallow answer. Do not answer the request, state\nfacts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. First inventory wholly distinct domain\nfamilies, reader-facing vocabulary, named methods, motivating mechanisms, and\ncanonical scenarios. Do not spend an item refining a proof or approach when a\nmajor concept family is still absent. Check the request for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: prefer the smallest scope that\n  fully satisfies repeated must-haves over optional breadth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request, but prefer a missing archetype family over a deeper\nversion of something the request already makes obvious.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"short operational requirement\",\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most five additions, ranked by importance. Each addition may contain\nat most three search leads. Use verdict \"pass\" when the plan is already\ncomplete enough. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nI'm currently working on a project. The professor wasn't clear, she just said that I will use NLP techniques on some data, de-identified Electronic Health Records, to extract Asian subgroups, and try to abstract the same subgroups as are in CDC WONDER data. Then, I'll apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases. I have no clue what she means. Can you first help explain what she might mean? I don't know examples of what Electronic Health Records look like. What NLP techniques might I be using on data like this? What subgroups in CDC WONDER data would I be abstracting? How could I apply this to look at prevalence rates of autoimmune diseases? What skills should I brush up on? Please help me create a plan for my research going forward, according to what the professor said.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"Identify the exact CDC WONDER dataset, years, geography, and race/ethnicity fields, then build a documented crosswalk from its Asian subgroup taxonomy to the labels recoverable from the EHR.\",\"reason\":\"CDC WONDER is a collection of datasets whose categories and coding change by source and period; without fixing the target taxonomy, “the same subgroups” is undefined and the extraction task cannot be validated.\",\"search_leads\":[\"CDC WONDER detailed race Asian subgroup categories dataset years\",\"CDC WONDER race ethnicity variable documentation bridged single race\",\"CDC WONDER data suppression subgroup counts\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Show a concrete de-identified EHR example spanning structured fields and free-text notes, and define “abstract” as creation of a standardized subgroup variable from those sources.\",\"reason\":\"A novice needs to see where subgroup evidence might reside—demographics, registration fields, social history, language, birthplace, and notes—and must distinguish explicit self-identification from unreliable inference based on names, language, or nationality.\",\"search_leads\":[\"EHR structured data versus clinical notes example FHIR\",\"OMOP person observation race ethnicity vocabulary\",\"clinical NLP race ethnicity extraction EHR notes\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Specify an end-to-end extraction and validation workflow: annotation guidelines, lexicon/rule baseline, named-entity or text-classification alternatives, negation/context handling, train-validation-test separation, clinician or dual-review adjudication, and subgroup-specific precision, recall, and F1 reporting.\",\"reason\":\"Listing generic NLP methods is insufficient; extraction can confuse patient identity with family history, clinician identity, negated statements, or historical text, and aggregate accuracy can conceal severe errors for small subgroups.\",\"search_leads\":[\"clinical NLP ethnicity extraction annotation guidelines validation\",\"spaCy medspaCy negation context clinical text\",\"subgroup fairness precision recall clinical NLP\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Define the autoimmune disease phenotypes and prevalence estimand before analysis, including case criteria using ICD/SNOMED codes, medications, labs, and notes; denominator, observation window, repeated patients, age/sex adjustment, confidence intervals, and sensitivity analyses.\",\"reason\":\"CDC WONDER outcomes may not directly measure disease prevalence, while EHR prevalence is sensitive to healthcare access, follow-up, coding, and phenotype definitions; numerator and denominator choices determine whether the resulting rates are interpretable or comparable.\",\"search_leads\":[\"validated EHR phenotype autoimmune disease ICD codes prevalence\",\"electronic health record prevalence denominator observation period\",\"CDC WONDER mortality versus morbidity prevalence limitations\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Include governance and feasibility gates—IRB and data-use approval, HIPAA/de-identification constraints, secure computing, minimum-cell and suppression rules, prohibition on re-identification—and a staged skills/tool plan using SQL, Python or R, regex, pandas, spaCy/medspaCy, coding vocabularies, and reproducible validation.\",\"reason\":\"Asian subgroup information is sensitive and sparse; privacy rules, missingness, selection bias, label provenance, and small samples may prevent the intended analysis or make apparent disparities misleading unless assessed before model building.\",\"search_leads\":[\"HIPAA de-identified EHR NLP IRB data use agreement\",\"clinical NLP secure environment protected health information\",\"race ethnicity EHR missingness misclassification small sample bias\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"requirement": "Identify the exact CDC WONDER dataset, years, geography, and race/ethnicity fields, then build a documented crosswalk from its Asian subgroup taxonomy to the labels recoverable from the EHR.", "reason": "CDC WONDER is a collection of datasets whose categories and coding change by source and period; without fixing the target taxonomy, “the same subgroups” is undefined and the extraction task cannot be validated.", "search_leads": ["CDC WONDER detailed race Asian subgroup categories dataset years", "CDC WONDER race ethnicity variable documentation bridged single race", "CDC WONDER data suppression subgroup counts"]}, {"requirement": "Show a concrete de-identified EHR example spanning structured fields and free-text notes, and define “abstract” as creation of a standardized subgroup variable from those sources.", "reason": "A novice needs to see where subgroup evidence might reside—demographics, registration fields, social history, language, birthplace, and notes—and must distinguish explicit self-identification from unreliable inference based on names, language, or nationality.", "search_leads": ["EHR structured data versus clinical notes example FHIR", "OMOP person observation race ethnicity vocabulary", "clinical NLP race ethnicity extraction EHR notes"]}, {"requirement": "Specify an end-to-end extraction and validation workflow: annotation guidelines, lexicon/rule baseline, named-entity or text-classification alternatives, negation/context handling, train-validation-test separation, clinician or dual-review adjudication, and subgroup-specific precision, recall, and F1 reporting.", "reason": "Listing generic NLP methods is insufficient; extraction can confuse patient identity with family history, clinician identity, negated statements, or historical text, and aggregate accuracy can conceal severe errors for small subgroups.", "search_leads": ["clinical NLP ethnicity extraction annotation guidelines validation", "spaCy medspaCy negation context clinical text", "subgroup fairness precision recall clinical NLP"]}, {"requirement": "Define the autoimmune disease phenotypes and prevalence estimand before analysis, including case criteria using ICD/SNOMED codes, medications, labs, and notes; denominator, observation window, repeated patients, age/sex adjustment, confidence intervals, and sensitivity analyses.", "reason": "CDC WONDER outcomes may not directly measure disease prevalence, while EHR prevalence is sensitive to healthcare access, follow-up, coding, and phenotype definitions; numerator and denominator choices determine whether the resulting rates are interpretable or comparable.", "search_leads": ["validated EHR phenotype autoimmune disease ICD codes prevalence", "electronic health record prevalence denominator observation period", "CDC WONDER mortality versus morbidity prevalence limitations"]}, {"requirement": "Include governance and feasibility gates—IRB and data-use approval, HIPAA/de-identification constraints, secure computing, minimum-cell and suppression rules, prohibition on re-identification—and a staged skills/tool plan using SQL, Python or R, regex, pandas, spaCy/medspaCy, coding vocabularies, and reproducible validation.", "reason": "Asian subgroup information is sensitive and sparse; privacy rules, missingness, selection bias, label provenance, and small samples may prevent the intended analysis or make apparent disparities misleading unless assessed before model building.", "search_leads": ["HIPAA de-identified EHR NLP IRB data use agreement", "clinical NLP secure environment protected health information", "race ethnicity EHR missingness misclassification small sample bias"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 991, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 982}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605440", "query": "Prove definitely whether a self-improving artificial intelligence system can be mathematically guaranteed to maintain alignment with human values indefinitely through recursive self-modification.", "system": "You are the blind coverage scout for a research system. You receive the\noriginal request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output. Build\nan independent inventory of the material a knowledgeable reader would find\nconspicuously missing from a shallow answer. Do not answer the request, state\nfacts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. First inventory wholly distinct domain\nfamilies, reader-facing vocabulary, named methods, motivating mechanisms, and\ncanonical scenarios. Do not spend an item refining a proof or approach when a\nmajor concept family is still absent. Check the request for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: prefer the smallest scope that\n  fully satisfies repeated must-haves over optional breadth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request, but prefer a missing archetype family over a deeper\nversion of something the request already makes obvious.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"short operational requirement\",\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most five additions, ranked by importance. Each addition may contain\nat most three search leads. Use verdict \"pass\" when the plan is already\ncomplete enough. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nProve definitely whether a self-improving artificial intelligence system can be mathematically guaranteed to maintain alignment with human values indefinitely through recursive self-modification.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"Formalize every quantifier and key term: system class, recursive self-modification, alignment, human values, indefinite duration, environment assumptions, and whether the guarantee is universal or conditional.\",\"reason\":\"Without a precise specification, “definitely” can conflate existence of one alignment-preserving design with a guarantee covering all systems, environments, and evolving human preferences.\",\"search_leads\":[\"formal definitions AI alignment recursive self-modification\",\"utility preservation self-modifying agents\",\"value learning changing human preferences formalization\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Evaluate the canonical limits on self-verification and program behavior, including Gödel incompleteness, Löb’s theorem, the halting problem, Rice’s theorem, and Vingean reflection, stating the assumptions and relevance of each.\",\"reason\":\"A possibility proof must address the standard mathematical barriers rather than relying on a generic appeal to undecidability or incompleteness.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Löb theorem self-modifying agents Vingean reflection\",\"Rice theorem verification AI alignment\",\"Gödel incompleteness proof-carrying self modification\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Separate unconditional guarantees from restricted constructions, and provide either a formal counterexample to the universal claim or a theorem for a precisely bounded architecture, with assumptions made explicit.\",\"reason\":\"This distinction is necessary to avoid overclaiming from a result that applies only to fixed utility functions, decidable modification classes, controlled environments, or trusted proof systems.\",\"search_leads\":[\"tiling agents formal self modification\",\"Gödel machine global optimality theorem assumptions\",\"proof carrying code self modifying systems\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Cover the principal alignment-loss mechanisms and canonical scenarios: Goodhart effects, specification gaming, mesa-optimization, goal misgeneralization, wireheading, corrigibility failure, ontological shifts, distribution shift, and adversarial modification.\",\"reason\":\"A purely syntactic proof-preservation argument may fail to capture whether the preserved formal objective continues to represent human values in the world.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Goodhart specification gaming mesa optimization alignment\",\"corrigibility self modification formal agents\",\"ontological crisis utility functions AI\"]},{\"requirement\":\"State boundary conditions involving finite computation, proof-system soundness, model uncertainty, open environments, changing or conflicting human values, multi-agent interaction, and hardware or implementation faults; conclude with a scoped result rather than an unrestricted slogan.\",\"reason\":\"Indefinite guarantees depend not only on logical preservation but also on resource, epistemic, social, and physical assumptions that determine what the theorem actually covers.\",\"search_leads\":[\"embedded agency logical uncertainty resource bounded agents\",\"AI value learning preference uncertainty changing values\",\"formal verification hardware faults open systems limitations\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"requirement": "Formalize every quantifier and key term: system class, recursive self-modification, alignment, human values, indefinite duration, environment assumptions, and whether the guarantee is universal or conditional.", "reason": "Without a precise specification, “definitely” can conflate existence of one alignment-preserving design with a guarantee covering all systems, environments, and evolving human preferences.", "search_leads": ["formal definitions AI alignment recursive self-modification", "utility preservation self-modifying agents", "value learning changing human preferences formalization"]}, {"requirement": "Evaluate the canonical limits on self-verification and program behavior, including Gödel incompleteness, Löb’s theorem, the halting problem, Rice’s theorem, and Vingean reflection, stating the assumptions and relevance of each.", "reason": "A possibility proof must address the standard mathematical barriers rather than relying on a generic appeal to undecidability or incompleteness.", "search_leads": ["Löb theorem self-modifying agents Vingean reflection", "Rice theorem verification AI alignment", "Gödel incompleteness proof-carrying self modification"]}, {"requirement": "Separate unconditional guarantees from restricted constructions, and provide either a formal counterexample to the universal claim or a theorem for a precisely bounded architecture, with assumptions made explicit.", "reason": "This distinction is necessary to avoid overclaiming from a result that applies only to fixed utility functions, decidable modification classes, controlled environments, or trusted proof systems.", "search_leads": ["tiling agents formal self modification", "Gödel machine global optimality theorem assumptions", "proof carrying code self modifying systems"]}, {"requirement": "Cover the principal alignment-loss mechanisms and canonical scenarios: Goodhart effects, specification gaming, mesa-optimization, goal misgeneralization, wireheading, corrigibility failure, ontological shifts, distribution shift, and adversarial modification.", "reason": "A purely syntactic proof-preservation argument may fail to capture whether the preserved formal objective continues to represent human values in the world.", "search_leads": ["Goodhart specification gaming mesa optimization alignment", "corrigibility self modification formal agents", "ontological crisis utility functions AI"]}, {"requirement": "State boundary conditions involving finite computation, proof-system soundness, model uncertainty, open environments, changing or conflicting human values, multi-agent interaction, and hardware or implementation faults; conclude with a scoped result rather than an unrestricted slogan.", "reason": "Indefinite guarantees depend not only on logical preservation but also on resource, epistemic, social, and physical assumptions that determine what the theorem actually covers.", "search_leads": ["embedded agency logical uncertainty resource bounded agents", "AI value learning preference uncertainty changing values", "formal verification hardware faults open systems limitations"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 856, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 894}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6054be", "query": "write a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.", "system": "You are the blind coverage scout for a research system. You receive the\noriginal request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output. Build\nan independent inventory of the material a knowledgeable reader would find\nconspicuously missing from a shallow answer. Do not answer the request, state\nfacts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. First inventory wholly distinct domain\nfamilies, reader-facing vocabulary, named methods, motivating mechanisms, and\ncanonical scenarios. Do not spend an item refining a proof or approach when a\nmajor concept family is still absent. Check the request for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: prefer the smallest scope that\n  fully satisfies repeated must-haves over optional breadth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request, but prefer a missing archetype family over a deeper\nversion of something the request already makes obvious.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"short operational requirement\",\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most five additions, ranked by importance. Each addition may contain\nat most three search leads. Use verdict \"pass\" when the plan is already\ncomplete enough. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nwrite a series of blog posts on important combinatorics topics in math olympiads. Include intuitive and detailed explanations, as well as example problems from top olympiads such as USAMO, USA TSTST, CMO, IMO, IMO shortlist, etc. Provide correct solutions for those problems and the insight or takeaways from those solutions.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"Define an explicit multi-post progression spanning the main olympiad-combinatorics families: foundational counting and bijections, pigeonhole and double counting, recurrences or generating functions, invariants and colorings, graph theory, extremal set systems, and probabilistic methods, with prerequisites and reuse of earlier techniques.\",\"reason\":\"Without a structured syllabus, a series can become a disconnected collection of tricks and omit entire standard branches of olympiad combinatorics.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad combinatorics syllabus major methods\",\"IMO combinatorics topic classification\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Give every post intuitive definitions, the mechanism behind each named method, guidance for recognizing when it applies, at least one alternative viewpoint or solution, and common failed approaches or boundary conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Detailed solutions alone do not provide the transferable problem-selection judgment or failure awareness expected from a tutorial series.\",\"search_leads\":[\"combinatorics olympiad problem solving heuristics\",\"common mistakes olympiad combinatorics\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Use accurately attributed problems from multiple requested competitions, identifying year and problem or shortlist code, preserving or clearly paraphrasing the conditions, and independently checking each complete solution against an authoritative source.\",\"reason\":\"Incorrect attribution, subtly altered hypotheses, and elegant but incomplete arguments are especially conspicuous in olympiad content.\",\"search_leads\":[\"IMO official problems solutions combinatorics\",\"USAMO TSTST official problems solutions\",\"CMO official problems solutions\"]},{\"requirement\":\"For each worked problem, separate discovery insight from formal proof, explicitly justify extremal choices, equality cases, constructions, and termination or counting claims, then end with reusable takeaways and nearby variations.\",\"reason\":\"These are common logical weak points in combinatorics proofs, and the requested insight is lost if the presentation only reproduces a polished official solution.\",\"search_leads\":[\"olympiad combinatorics extremal principle proof\",\"combinatorics equality cases constructions olympiad\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Balance the examples across difficulty levels and mechanisms, including constructive versus impossibility problems and at least one explicit transfer between representations such as sets, graphs, colorings, sequences, or counting arguments.\",\"reason\":\"A useful series should teach escalation and model selection rather than repeatedly showcasing one familiar technique or competition level.\",\"search_leads\":[\"IMO shortlist combinatorics constructions colorings graphs\",\"olympiad combinatorics multiple solutions\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"requirement": "Define an explicit multi-post progression spanning the main olympiad-combinatorics families: foundational counting and bijections, pigeonhole and double counting, recurrences or generating functions, invariants and colorings, graph theory, extremal set systems, and probabilistic methods, with prerequisites and reuse of earlier techniques.", "reason": "Without a structured syllabus, a series can become a disconnected collection of tricks and omit entire standard branches of olympiad combinatorics.", "search_leads": ["olympiad combinatorics syllabus major methods", "IMO combinatorics topic classification"]}, {"requirement": "Give every post intuitive definitions, the mechanism behind each named method, guidance for recognizing when it applies, at least one alternative viewpoint or solution, and common failed approaches or boundary conditions.", "reason": "Detailed solutions alone do not provide the transferable problem-selection judgment or failure awareness expected from a tutorial series.", "search_leads": ["combinatorics olympiad problem solving heuristics", "common mistakes olympiad combinatorics"]}, {"requirement": "Use accurately attributed problems from multiple requested competitions, identifying year and problem or shortlist code, preserving or clearly paraphrasing the conditions, and independently checking each complete solution against an authoritative source.", "reason": "Incorrect attribution, subtly altered hypotheses, and elegant but incomplete arguments are especially conspicuous in olympiad content.", "search_leads": ["IMO official problems solutions combinatorics", "USAMO TSTST official problems solutions", "CMO official problems solutions"]}, {"requirement": "For each worked problem, separate discovery insight from formal proof, explicitly justify extremal choices, equality cases, constructions, and termination or counting claims, then end with reusable takeaways and nearby variations.", "reason": "These are common logical weak points in combinatorics proofs, and the requested insight is lost if the presentation only reproduces a polished official solution.", "search_leads": ["olympiad combinatorics extremal principle proof", "combinatorics equality cases constructions olympiad"]}, {"requirement": "Balance the examples across difficulty levels and mechanisms, including constructive versus impossibility problems and at least one explicit transfer between representations such as sets, graphs, colorings, sequences, or counting arguments.", "reason": "A useful series should teach escalation and model selection rather than repeatedly showcasing one familiar technique or competition level.", "search_leads": ["IMO shortlist combinatorics constructions colorings graphs", "olympiad combinatorics multiple solutions"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 897, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 760}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a6053fb", "query": "Conduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.", "system": "You are the blind coverage scout for a research system. You receive the\noriginal request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output. Build\nan independent inventory of the material a knowledgeable reader would find\nconspicuously missing from a shallow answer. Do not answer the request, state\nfacts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. First inventory wholly distinct domain\nfamilies, reader-facing vocabulary, named methods, motivating mechanisms, and\ncanonical scenarios. Do not spend an item refining a proof or approach when a\nmajor concept family is still absent. Check the request for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: prefer the smallest scope that\n  fully satisfies repeated must-haves over optional breadth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request, but prefer a missing archetype family over a deeper\nversion of something the request already makes obvious.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"short operational requirement\",\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most five additions, ranked by importance. Each addition may contain\nat most three search leads. Use verdict \"pass\" when the plan is already\ncomplete enough. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nConduct an analysis to determine whether social media's overall impact on society has been more beneficial or detrimental. Argument should be structured by examining social media's impact across key domains of society. For each domain you must evaluate both positive and negative effects, using evidence from academic studies, journalist reports, case studies. After analyzing each domain, construct a definitive judgment that weighs these impacts on each other.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"Define “social media,” “society,” the geographic and time scope, and the counterfactual against which “beneficial or detrimental” will be judged.\",\"reason\":\"Without explicit boundaries and a baseline, the overall judgment becomes ambiguous and may improperly generalize across eras, platforms, and populations.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media societal impact longitudinal review definition counterfactual\",\"global social media use historical platform evolution\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Cover a compact but comprehensive domain set: mental and physical health; relationships and community; politics, civic life, and information quality; economy, work, and creators; education and knowledge; and privacy, safety, and inequality.\",\"reason\":\"A society-wide conclusion is not credible if it concentrates on a few prominent harms or benefits while omitting major institutional and individual domains.\",\"search_leads\":[\"systematic review social media societal impacts domains\",\"social media benefits harms multidisciplinary review\"]},{\"requirement\":\"For every domain, identify the mechanisms producing outcomes—such as algorithmic ranking, virality, social metrics, targeted advertising, network effects, moderation, and user-generated content—and include academic evidence, a reported investigation, and a concrete case study representing both benefits and harms.\",\"reason\":\"Mechanisms make the analysis explanatory rather than a list of correlations, while the requested evidence mix must be applied consistently rather than appearing only in isolated sections.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media affordances mechanisms algorithmic amplification review\",\"social media case studies benefits harms journalism academic\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Assess evidence quality and heterogeneity, distinguishing correlation from causal inference and comparing effects by age, gender, socioeconomic status, region, platform, and type or intensity of use; include non-Western cases and contrasting regulatory settings.\",\"reason\":\"Average effects can conceal unequal or opposing outcomes, and a predominantly Western or single-platform evidence base cannot sustain a judgment about society overall.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social media causal inference longitudinal experiments meta-analysis\",\"social media effects Global South comparative study\",\"social media regulation comparative platforms society\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Use an explicit synthesis rubric to reach the definitive judgment, weighing magnitude, prevalence, duration, reversibility, distribution, and confidence in the evidence; distinguish short- from long-run effects and state boundary conditions and uncertainty.\",\"reason\":\"A final verdict cannot follow merely by counting positive and negative examples; it needs transparent rules for comparing qualitatively different impacts and identifying who gains, who bears costs, and under what conditions.\",\"search_leads\":[\"social impact assessment weighting magnitude distribution reversibility\",\"social media net societal impact cost benefit framework\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"requirement": "Define “social media,” “society,” the geographic and time scope, and the counterfactual against which “beneficial or detrimental” will be judged.", "reason": "Without explicit boundaries and a baseline, the overall judgment becomes ambiguous and may improperly generalize across eras, platforms, and populations.", "search_leads": ["social media societal impact longitudinal review definition counterfactual", "global social media use historical platform evolution"]}, {"requirement": "Cover a compact but comprehensive domain set: mental and physical health; relationships and community; politics, civic life, and information quality; economy, work, and creators; education and knowledge; and privacy, safety, and inequality.", "reason": "A society-wide conclusion is not credible if it concentrates on a few prominent harms or benefits while omitting major institutional and individual domains.", "search_leads": ["systematic review social media societal impacts domains", "social media benefits harms multidisciplinary review"]}, {"requirement": "For every domain, identify the mechanisms producing outcomes—such as algorithmic ranking, virality, social metrics, targeted advertising, network effects, moderation, and user-generated content—and include academic evidence, a reported investigation, and a concrete case study representing both benefits and harms.", "reason": "Mechanisms make the analysis explanatory rather than a list of correlations, while the requested evidence mix must be applied consistently rather than appearing only in isolated sections.", "search_leads": ["social media affordances mechanisms algorithmic amplification review", "social media case studies benefits harms journalism academic"]}, {"requirement": "Assess evidence quality and heterogeneity, distinguishing correlation from causal inference and comparing effects by age, gender, socioeconomic status, region, platform, and type or intensity of use; include non-Western cases and contrasting regulatory settings.", "reason": "Average effects can conceal unequal or opposing outcomes, and a predominantly Western or single-platform evidence base cannot sustain a judgment about society overall.", "search_leads": ["social media causal inference longitudinal experiments meta-analysis", "social media effects Global South comparative study", "social media regulation comparative platforms society"]}, {"requirement": "Use an explicit synthesis rubric to reach the definitive judgment, weighing magnitude, prevalence, duration, reversibility, distribution, and confidence in the evidence; distinguish short- from long-run effects and state boundary conditions and uncertainty.", "reason": "A final verdict cannot follow merely by counting positive and negative examples; it needs transparent rules for comparing qualitatively different impacts and identifying who gains, who bears costs, and under what conditions.", "search_leads": ["social impact assessment weighting magnitude distribution reversibility", "social media net societal impact cost benefit framework"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 903, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 882}}
{"qid": "6847465956a0f6376a605391", "query": "Write a strategic business report advising a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup on how to enter and scale in the Southeast Asian market (focusing on countries like Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia). Your report should address: 1. A comparative analysis of consumer demand trends for alternative proteins in each target country. 2. Competitive landscape: key regional players, pricing, retail/distribution channels, and regulatory hurdles. 3. Cultural and dietary considerations that may influence adoption and positioning. 4. Go-to-market recommendations such as direct-to-consumer, B2B partnerships, and quick service restaurants. 5. Risks and opportunities associated with regulatory shifts, supply chain localization, and sustainability branding. Use regional market data, public reports, consumer surveys, and industry benchmarks. Assume your audience is a board of venture capital-backed startup executives seeking a data-driven, execution-ready plan.", "system": "You are the blind coverage scout for a research system. You receive the\noriginal request but deliberately do not see the other planner's output. Build\nan independent inventory of the material a knowledgeable reader would find\nconspicuously missing from a shallow answer. Do not answer the request, state\nfacts, or treat your own knowledge as evidence.\n\nCoverage diversity comes before depth. First inventory wholly distinct domain\nfamilies, reader-facing vocabulary, named methods, motivating mechanisms, and\ncanonical scenarios. Do not spend an item refining a proof or approach when a\nmajor concept family is still absent. Check the request for:\n- canonical concepts and terminology needed to frame the domain completely,\n  including qualitative mechanisms and risk dynamics rather than only formal\n  machinery;\n- standard alternatives, mechanisms, institutions, dimensions, or competing\n  approaches that the plan overlooked;\n- concrete examples, counterexamples, prior measurements, or evidence types\n  needed to turn an abstract answer into a useful one;\n- failure scenarios, boundary conditions, resource limits, and credible\n  mitigations for a claim about what is possible or guaranteed;\n- every repeated deliverable requirement, prerequisite, definition, variation,\n  alternative viewpoint, transferable insight, and progression step in a\n  tutorial, series, or other pedagogical request; and\n- feasibility under the 1,024-word answer cap: prefer the smallest scope that\n  fully satisfies repeated must-haves over optional breadth.\n\nFor a broad named domain or problem, actively recall the small set of standard\nterms, mechanisms, and illustrative cases a serious introductory treatment is\nnormally expected to cover. For a series on important topics, inventory major\nbranches of the field, prerequisites and variations for each part, at least one\nalternative viewpoint and cross-domain transfer, and an explicit progression\nthat reuses earlier ideas. For a conceptual safety or possibility argument,\ninventory both the formal limits and the standard motivating dynamics,\nfailure scenarios, learning approaches, thought experiments, and resource\nconstraints.\n\nMatch the request to its answer archetype and reserve coverage for the compact\nexpectations that type routinely hides:\n- A novice research guide normally needs expanded acronyms, named usable tools,\n  the exact governing privacy or validation mechanisms, a concrete end-to-end\n  workflow, and enough domain examples to make the proposed measurement real.\n- A board strategy normally needs a defensible and measurable product edge,\n  accountable roles and operating model, capital and unit-economics gates,\n  long-term strategic or exit options, and named government programs material\n  to positioning.\n- A society-wide judgment normally needs justified geography and time scope,\n  non-Western cases, technological turning points and platform mechanisms,\n  regulatory contrasts at relevant levels, and short- versus long-run effects.\n- A proof or possibility answer normally needs the field's canonical named\n  limits, definitions, qualitative dynamics, and scenarios, not only a valid\n  formal argument.\n- A tutorial series normally needs the field's major branches, intuitive\n  definitions in every part, at least one alternative solution or viewpoint,\n  common failure modes, and one explicit cross-domain transfer.\n\nThese are archetype prompts, not a checklist to copy. Select only expectations\nmaterial to this request, but prefer a missing archetype family over a deeper\nversion of something the request already makes obvious.\n\nDo not propose fewer parts, examples, cases, or other deliverables. Do not\nrelax or contradict the request. Add only high-value coverage; do not produce a\nniche wishlist.\n\nReturn JSON only in exactly one of these shapes:\n{\"verdict\":\"pass\",\"additions\":[]}\n{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"short operational requirement\",\"reason\":\"why its absence is material\",\"search_leads\":[\"compact retrieval query\"]}]}\n\nReturn at most five additions, ranked by importance. Each addition may contain\nat most three search leads. Use verdict \"pass\" when the plan is already\ncomplete enough. Do not wrap the JSON in Markdown.\n", "request": "ORIGINAL RESEARCH REQUEST\n\nWrite a strategic business report advising a mid-stage U.S. plant-based meat startup on how to enter and scale in the Southeast Asian market (focusing on countries like Singapore, Thailand, and Indonesia). Your report should address: 1. A comparative analysis of consumer demand trends for alternative proteins in each target country. 2. Competitive landscape: key regional players, pricing, retail/distribution channels, and regulatory hurdles. 3. Cultural and dietary considerations that may influence adoption and positioning. 4. Go-to-market recommendations such as direct-to-consumer, B2B partnerships, and quick service restaurants. 5. Risks and opportunities associated with regulatory shifts, supply chain localization, and sustainability branding. Use regional market data, public reports, consumer surveys, and industry benchmarks. Assume your audience is a board of venture capital-backed startup executives seeking a data-driven, execution-ready plan.", "raw": "{\"verdict\":\"add\",\"additions\":[{\"requirement\":\"Provide a phased country-entry sequence with explicit 12-, 24-, and 36-month milestones, market-selection scorecard, investment requirements, accountable owners, and go/no-go gates.\",\"reason\":\"A board needs an executable capital-allocation roadmap, not merely channel recommendations; sequencing is essential because readiness, cost, and regulatory complexity differ by market.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia alternative protein market entry benchmarks\",\"Southeast Asia plant based food distributor QSR partnership case study\",\"alternative protein startup international expansion KPI benchmarks\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Build country-level unit economics and market sizing, including TAM/SAM/SOM, landed cost, target retail and foodservice pricing, distributor and retailer margins, customer-acquisition cost, repeat purchase, contribution margin, and break-even scenarios.\",\"reason\":\"Pricing comparisons alone cannot establish commercial viability or define measurable product-market-fit and capital-efficiency gates for a venture-backed company.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia plant based meat prices retail foodservice\",\"Southeast Asia grocery distributor retailer margin benchmarks\",\"plant based meat unit economics CAC repeat purchase benchmark\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Map the exact regulatory and certification pathway in each country, naming responsible agencies and covering product classification, import approval, labeling and claims, food safety, novel ingredients, halal certification, and likely approval timelines and costs.\",\"reason\":\"Generic regulatory-hurdle coverage would not support launch planning, especially where formulation, religious certification, packaging, and importer requirements can affect both addressable demand and time to market.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Singapore SFA alternative protein import labeling novel food rules\",\"Thailand FDA plant based meat import labeling regulation\",\"Indonesia BPOM halal plant based food import requirements\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Define the localized product and positioning strategy by country: formats and flavors, cooking applications, protein sources, nutrition targets, pack sizes, price tiers, sensory testing, and claims architecture, with a structured pilot-and-learn plan.\",\"reason\":\"Cultural observations become actionable only when translated into product specifications, willingness-to-pay tests, target segments, and measurable trial-to-repeat experiments.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia plant based meat consumer survey taste price health\",\"Southeast Asia alternative protein sensory preference study\",\"Asian plant based meat consumer segmentation flexitarian survey\"]},{\"requirement\":\"Recommend an operating model and defensible regional advantage, including local manufacturing versus export scenarios, co-manufacturers and ingredient sourcing, cold-chain needs, quality control, IP protection, key hires and partners, resilience contingencies, and longer-term strategic options.\",\"reason\":\"The board must understand how localization changes margins and risk, who will execute the plan, and whether the expansion can create a durable edge rather than easily replicated distribution.\",\"search_leads\":[\"Southeast Asia plant protein ingredient supply co manufacturing cold chain\",\"Singapore Thailand Indonesia food manufacturing incentives alternative protein\",\"Southeast Asia food startup joint venture distributor operating model\"]}]}", "normalized": {"verdict": "add", "additions": [{"requirement": "Provide a phased country-entry sequence with explicit 12-, 24-, and 36-month milestones, market-selection scorecard, investment requirements, accountable owners, and go/no-go gates.", "reason": "A board needs an executable capital-allocation roadmap, not merely channel recommendations; sequencing is essential because readiness, cost, and regulatory complexity differ by market.", "search_leads": ["Singapore Thailand Indonesia alternative protein market entry benchmarks", "Southeast Asia plant based food distributor QSR partnership case study", "alternative protein startup international expansion KPI benchmarks"]}, {"requirement": "Build country-level unit economics and market sizing, including TAM/SAM/SOM, landed cost, target retail and foodservice pricing, distributor and retailer margins, customer-acquisition cost, repeat purchase, contribution margin, and break-even scenarios.", "reason": "Pricing comparisons alone cannot establish commercial viability or define measurable product-market-fit and capital-efficiency gates for a venture-backed company.", "search_leads": ["Singapore Thailand Indonesia plant based meat prices retail foodservice", "Southeast Asia grocery distributor retailer margin benchmarks", "plant based meat unit economics CAC repeat purchase benchmark"]}, {"requirement": "Map the exact regulatory and certification pathway in each country, naming responsible agencies and covering product classification, import approval, labeling and claims, food safety, novel ingredients, halal certification, and likely approval timelines and costs.", "reason": "Generic regulatory-hurdle coverage would not support launch planning, especially where formulation, religious certification, packaging, and importer requirements can affect both addressable demand and time to market.", "search_leads": ["Singapore SFA alternative protein import labeling novel food rules", "Thailand FDA plant based meat import labeling regulation", "Indonesia BPOM halal plant based food import requirements"]}, {"requirement": "Define the localized product and positioning strategy by country: formats and flavors, cooking applications, protein sources, nutrition targets, pack sizes, price tiers, sensory testing, and claims architecture, with a structured pilot-and-learn plan.", "reason": "Cultural observations become actionable only when translated into product specifications, willingness-to-pay tests, target segments, and measurable trial-to-repeat experiments.", "search_leads": ["Singapore Thailand Indonesia plant based meat consumer survey taste price health", "Southeast Asia alternative protein sensory preference study", "Asian plant based meat consumer segmentation flexitarian survey"]}, {"requirement": "Recommend an operating model and defensible regional advantage, including local manufacturing versus export scenarios, co-manufacturers and ingredient sourcing, cold-chain needs, quality control, IP protection, key hires and partners, resilience contingencies, and longer-term strategic options.", "reason": "The board must understand how localization changes margins and risk, who will execute the plan, and whether the expansion can create a durable edge rather than easily replicated distribution.", "search_leads": ["Southeast Asia plant protein ingredient supply co manufacturing cold chain", "Singapore Thailand Indonesia food manufacturing incentives alternative protein", "Southeast Asia food startup joint venture distributor operating model"]}], "parse_error": false}, "usage": {"input_tokens": 1003, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 778}}
